      "id": "evt_nyu_physics_event7580",
      "name": "What Are We Learning About Super-Eddington Accretion Disks From Simulations?",
      "description": "Accretion of gas onto gravitationally compact objects (black holes specifically) is one of the most important processes acting to shape our Universe. At extremely high rates of accretion (dubbed `super-Eddington'), the huge amount of liberated energy in the form of radiation, results in the accretion disk becoming geometrically thick, with mass lost via winds. Understanding this super-Eddington regime of accretion is vital to explaining the challenging observation that supermassive black holes (SMBHs) are fully formed at redshifts >7. It is also important to understanding astrophysical events and objects such as tidal disruption events (TDEs) and ultra-luminous X-ray sources (ULXs). While we are able to perform observations of super-Eddington accreting systems, to understand them theoretically and make any progress, we must turn to numerical studies, as the interplay between strong radiation fields and the dynamics of magnetized fluids cannot be solved analytically, except under very restrictive assumptions. In this talk, I will present the results of some recent super-Eddington disk simulations and discuss some of the interesting things we are learning.",
      "start_date": "2025-12-02T14:00:00+00:00",
      "end_date": "2025-12-02T15:00:00+00:00",
      "location_id": null,
      "community_id": null,
