    {
      "id": "evt_newschool_5f67da28",
      "name": "BFA & MFA Fine Arts Open Studios",
      "type": "Exhibition",
      "location_id": "loc_newschool_main",
      "community_id": "com_newschool",
      "description": "MFA Graduate Studios39 West 13th Street between 5th and 6th AvenuesBFA Senior Studios25 East 13th Street between University Place and 5th Avenue, 4th Floor39 West 13th Street between 5th and 6th Avenues Parsons Fine Arts is thrilled to open its doors for an in-person, on-campus Fall 2025 MFA and BFA Open Studios! We welcome members of the public and The New School Community on Friday, December 12th from 5:00 pm to 8:00 pm at the studios at 25 East 13th Street between University Place and Fifth Avenue on the 4th Floor and at 39 West 13th Street between 5th and 6th Avenues.MFA Fine Arts Open Studios:The MFA Fine Arts program at Parsons is a rigorous, cross-disciplinary, two-year program supporting a diverse and international group of emerging artists to grow into their full potential as critical contributors to the field. The Open Studios allow visitors to gain insight into vibrant studio processes, artistic research, and innovative experimentation. Work presented reaches across a wide variety of media, concepts and ideas. Artists will be present to engage in informal conversations.MFA Artists:Moe Ali, Gabriela Awad, Max Azemar i Carnicero, Juli Badics, Zuzana Baková, Emilija Berga, Jackie Brady, Xianwen Chen, Yi Chen, Veronika Desova, Becca Dugas, Emma Fashing, MIL FIENCO, Vanda Focanti, Giovanna, Mckenna Goade, Kasia Hope, Karis Huang, Wei Huang, Jeehoo Kim, Yiseul LeMieux, Jayden Liu, Negin Pazandeh, Fidel Perez, Frantz Pierre, Marian Rodriguez, Shrujana Shridhar, Aida Sulova, Alekz Thoms, Juana Vargas Moreno, and Roro Wang.Parsons Fine Arts MFA is a dynamic two-year, cross-disciplinary program committed to expanding the formal, intellectual and conceptual dimensions of emerging artists’ work. We foster studio-based research and scholarship that extends the boundaries of contemporary cultural expression, developed through a global understanding of the arts. The program is committed to diversity among students and faculty that provides a potent learning community. Housed within both Parsons School of Design and The New School University, the Fine Arts program is uniquely positioned within a progressive educational environment. Our international student body has access to a wide spectrum of activities, ranging from rigorous formal and aesthetic investigations to cross-disciplinary collaborations with design, performing arts and humanities students, to public forums that address pressing social and political concerns.BFA Fine Arts Open Studios:The BFA Fine Arts program guides students to discover their artistic voice, familiarizing them with traditional painting, drawing, sculpture, and video techniques, as well as interdisciplinary methods that can include performance, multimedia installation, and virtual and time-based approaches.The program culminates in a transformative final year where students cultivate self-directed studio practices working toward a thesis exhibition. Over two semesters, they work in individual studios while engaging in weekly one-on-one and paired visits with practicing artist faculty. These collaborative exchanges support students in articulating the questions driving their work and develop the critical vocabulary needed to contextualize their practice within contemporary art discourse. Students learn to approach research as both rigorous and intuitive, drawing from theoretical texts, material experiments, and careful observation. Through midterm and final reviews with faculty panels and visiting critics, students present and defend their evolving practice, discovering how their work communicates beyond the academic context. This comprehensive approach prepares graduates to sustain meaningful artistic practices and contribute to the broader cultural conversation. BFA Artists: Jules Kresnicka, Violet Ruby, Helen Zhang, Lilyan Chu, Ting Xue, Erewyn Limric, Annie Winter, Sarah Thigpen, Sofia Losada, Joseph La Ve, August Payne, Autumn Henry, Gracie Jeffers, Othandiwe Ngubane, Ellie Kalman, Ruqing Yan, Jooyeon Lee, Maya Giurgi, Weiqin Li, Cookie Xia, Hailey Scheff, Fortune Laboz, Ayasha Siregar, Talia Bar-Tur, Kennedy Giovanell, Nicole Chen, Judas Knox, Maria Acosta Ramos, Sophia Zhao, Hazel Tanaka, Grace Burroughs, Hu Lim, Camy Brown, Lindsey Goldberg, Mia Frank, Ella Wingate, Ara Davis, Kristyna Lorenzo, Amalia Garcia, Martina Duque, Tori Cho, Zoe Wang, Daniel Wang, Alexis Rodriguez, Wen Trpisovsky, Tina Chen, Liz Roff, Ann Gui, Lola Roberts, and Ava Gordon.Parsons Fine Arts is home to an active, award-winning international faculty whose work has been included in the Whitney Biennial, Documenta, the Berlin Biennale and the 15th Gwangju Biennale; and exhibited in institutions such as the Kunstverein für die Rheinlande und Westfalen, Düsseldorf; ICA-Philadelphia, The Kitchen NYC and the Museum of Modern Art, New York. Additionally our Visiting Artist Lecture Series and our Critic and Curator Series features renowned, multidisciplinary artists, curators and critics who are available for one-on-one visits or intimate conversations with graduate students. This culture of dialogical exchange supports our graduate students to redefine the cutting edge of Contemporary Art. For more information, please visit amt.parsons.edu/finearts.For MFA information please contact Program Director Soyoung Yoon soyoung.yoon@newschool.edu.For BFA information please contact Director Catherine Telford Keogh telfordc@newschool.edu.",
