      "id": "evt_newschool_716f7700",
      "name": "Mannes Orchestra Fall 2025: Sexson, Martinů & Stravinsky",
      "type": "Performance",
      "location_id": "loc_newschool_main",
      "community_id": "com_newschool",
      "description": "Join Mannes Orchestra for a program that bridges legacy and contemporary innovation. The concert features Tyler Sexson, recipient of the prestigious Martinu Prize, whose dynamic orchestral work reflects the enduring influence of Bohuslav Martinů, a distinguished composer and former Mannes faculty member. The program also highlights the iconic music of Martinů himself and Igor Stravinsky. MANNES ORCHESTRATyler Sexson: Longmire Bohuslav Martinů: Memorial to LidiceINTERMISSION Igor Stravinsky: Petrushka (1947)Conductor: David HayesAbout the Program:  Tyler Sexson: LongmireDeep in the woods surrounding Mount Rainier, amongst the looming pines and moss-carpeted rock lies Longmire. Here, the clamor of everyday life fades away to a beautiful stillness unique to this remote scene. The entire landscape seems to be a study of all the possible shades of green and the near constant rainfall leaves every surface damp and glistening. When the sun does come, it falls in golden curtains through the tree canopy and stirs mist up from the forest floor. This is the world listeners of Longmire are invited to. Waves of orchestral textures churn over one another, emerging from murky depths and rising to bright vistas of clarity. The musical landscape mostly mirrors the overcast skies of the Pacific Northwest, though there are moments when the clouds part and the meadows of Longmire shimmer in the sunlight.  Bohuslav Martinů: Memorial to LidiceComposed in 1943 while in exile in the United States, Memorial to Lidice is a poignant orchestral elegy dedicated to the victims of the 1942 Nazi massacre of the Czech village of Lidice. The piece is structured in a single movement and is approximately nine minutes in length. Martinů employs the theme of fate from Beethoven's Fifth Symphony, symbolizing resistance against the Nazi invaders. The work was first performed in New York City in October 1943.Igor Stravinsky: Petrushka (1947)Stravinsky’s 1947 revision of Petrushka adapts his 1911 ballet for concert performance with a smaller orchestra. The revision preserves the colorful melodies, lively rhythms, and characterful themes of the original while making it more practical for orchestral performance.",
      "start_date": "2025-12-13T00:30:00+00:00",
      "end_date": "2025-12-13T02:30:00+00:00",
