    {
      "id": "evt_nyu_neuroscience_04dbc24f",
      "name": "Neuroscience Colloquium - Anne Schaefer, MD, PhD (ISMMS)",
      "description": "Monday, December 1, 2025, 12:30&nbsp;&ndash;&nbsp;1:30pm <br/><br/><strong>&#160;Title:</strong><em> &#8220;Adaptive&#8221; immunity by microglia</em><br /><br /><strong>Abstract:&#160;</strong>Microglia, the innate immune cells of the brain, play important roles in diverse brain functions. In addition to their role in the healthy brain, microglia responses can define the progression of Alzheimer&#8217;s disease (AD). The microglial response to amyloid plaques in AD can range from neuroprotective to neurotoxic. We have identified a distinct subpopulation of neuroprotective microglia, characterised by the expression of immunoregulatory lymphoid receptor proteins, that suppress brain-wide microglial inflammation and slow the buildup of amyloid plaques and toxic tau spreading&#8212;the hallmarks of Alzheimer&#8217;s disease.&#160; <br/><br/><b>NYU Location</b>:&nbsp;In person: Science Building, SB 103 <br/><b>Event Type</b>:&nbsp;Seminar / Seminar Series <br/><b>Sponsored by</b>:&nbsp;Department of Neuroscience <br/><b>Contact Name</b>:&nbsp;Rihan Hassad <br/><b>Contact Email</b>:&nbsp;<a href=\"mailto:Rihan.Hassad@nyulangone.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rihan.Hassad@nyulangone.org</a> <br/><b>Speaker #1 Name</b>:&nbsp;Anne Schaefer <br/><b>Speaker #1 Degree</b>:&nbsp;MD, PhD <br/><b>Speaker #1 Position</b>:&nbsp;Professor, Departments of Neuroscience, Psychiatry <br/><b>Speaker #1 Institution</b>:&nbsp;Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai <br/><b>Speaker #1 Additional Information</b>:&nbsp;Managing Director, MPI for Biology of Ageing <br/><b>Seminar Host(s)</b>:&nbsp;Faculty host: Dr. Shane Liddelow <br/><b>Pri. Seminar Sponsor</b>:&nbsp;Co-hosts: Priya Prakash PhD (Liddelow Lab) and Olivia Mosto (Klann Lab)",
