      "id": "evt_nyu_physics_event7555",
      "name": "Megavolcanoes, Pyrotornadoes, and Hurricanes: How Extreme Storms Can Influence Climate",
      "description": "The presence of water vapor in the upper atmosphere is enormously consequential for the climate. Strong thunderstorms are known to be an important secondary source of water vapor to the lower stratosphere, and how they may feedback to large scales in a warming climate is unknown. Extreme storms of very diverse type---supercell thunderstorms, hurricanes, pyrocumulonimbus, and volcanic plumes---are all able to pierce the lower boundary of the stratosphere and dump vast amounts of ice, water vapor and trace gases into the upper atmosphere. Many of these storms exhibit an Above-Anvil Cirrus Plume (AACP), which is a wake of ice and water vapor downstream of overshooting deep convection. The AACP is uniquely capable of lofting water high above the tropopause. Using high-resolution large eddy simulations, we show that the AACP is formed by the development of a new type of hydraulic jump at the tropopause, accompanied by windspeeds that exceed 110 m/s. The presence of a threshold past which some storms become effective hydrators of the lower stratosphere suggests a blind spot in large-scale climate models unable to resolve this behavior. The storm feedback on climate due to a humidified lower stratosphere is an exciting area of future research, which requires understanding the small-scale dynamics of severe weather.",
      "start_date": "2025-12-04T16:00:00+00:00",
      "end_date": "2025-12-04T17:00:00+00:00",
