    {
      "id": "evt_columbia_7a9874fa",
      "name": "CPRC Seminar Series with Professor Sara Heller",
      "type": "Seminar",
      "location_id": "loc_virtual",
      "community_id": "com_columbia_general",
      "description": "&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Longer-Term and Health Effects of an Experiment to Reduce Gun Violence &amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Community violence interventions aim to reduce shootings by providing services to those at highest risk of shooting or being shot. Our previous study of READI Chicago&amp;mdash;a program offering 18 months of supported work and cognitive behavioral therapy to men at high shooting risk&amp;mdash;showed mixed results during the 20-month program period, with large declines in shooting arrest and victimization among a subgroup referred by local outreach workers. This paper adds data through 40 months post-randomization, after programming ended, as well as hospital records providing both alternative violence measures and other health outcomes. The presentation will discuss 40-month results, including the persistence of previous crime results, new health outcomes, and updated benefit-cost comparisons.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sara Heller &lt;/strong&gt;is an Associate Professor of Economics at the University of Michigan. She studies interventions to reduce crime and improve other life outcomes among disadvantaged youth. She uses large-scale experiments to isolate the causal effects of a variety of programs, including cognitive behavioral therapy-based interventions and summer jobs.&lt;/p&gt;",
      "start_date": "2025-12-02T21:00:00+00:00",
      "end_date": "2025-12-02T22:00:00+00:00",
      "category": [
